4.1 Conversational-floor features
The conversational-floor features quantify how a participant oc-
cupies the conversational floor—how much they speak, how often
they seize or defend a turn, and how they overlap cooperatively
with others—and are the behavioural channel most directly linked
to perceived dominance in prior meeting corpora [2, 14].
4.1.1 Provenance. These features are derived from manually cor-
rected turn-level transcripts rather than from raw automatic output.
Each participant’s close-talk (DPA) channel was first transcribed
and diarised with WhisperX [3]—faster-whisper decoding followed
by wav2vec2 forced alignment—and then hand-verified against the
room audio to correct speaker attribution and segment boundaries.
In the corrected transcript every segment carries a type label—
speech (SPK), silence (SIL), overlap (OVL), backchannel (BCK), or
laughter (LAU)—and every overlap additionally carries an inter-
actional subtype (competitive, floor_fight, smooth, collaborative, or
backchannel_ovl). All conversational-floor features in Table 3 are
computed deterministically from these annotated {onset, duration,
speaker, type, subtype} fields, so the feature values are grounded in
the human-verified turn structure rather than in acoustic heuristics.
4.1.2 Turn and bout construction. A turn (floor-holding run) is
a maximal sequence of a speaker’s SPK segments merged across
silences of at most 0.3 s. Competitive interruptions are aggregated
into bouts: consecutive competitive bids whose onsets fall within
1.5 s are merged into a single contest episode whose initiator is the
first bidder. A bout is resolved by the first speaker to hold the floor
continuously for at least 2.0 s, starting within 6.0 s of bout onset;
